Gas Atomization
Gas atomization of titanium was originally developed by Crucible Materials Corporation in the
1980s.19,20 In this process, elemental raw materials or pre-alloyed titanium alloy ingots or bars are induction skull-melted in a water-cooled copper crucible under vacuum. Once the composition of alloy becomes homogenous after being held in the molten state for a certain period of time, the melt is poured into a refractory metal nozzle in a tundish and then atomized by high-pressure streams of inlet gas (Ar is usually chosen over helium for economic reasons). As the stream of molten metal falls freely as a result of gravitational forces for a certain distance before atomization, this technique is called free-fall gas atomization (FFGA). The basic configuration of the FFGA process is shown in Fig. 4a. The gas-to-metal ratio (G/M) is one of the important parameters that determines the PSD of the
gas-atomized powder.21 Lubanska developed an empirical equation for the relation between the d50
